record templartorrent.observation.complete-piece-status/9 : observation {
    title "Linux client with forked rbit (no ring), rate-ranked choking, multi-piece pipelining; remaining boundaries"
    state superseded
    statement """
        2026-10-22 late, Linux x86_64/Rust 1.99.0, committed on master through 3ed3709 plus the rate-ranking and multi-lease commit that follows. Since rev 8: rbit 0.2.2 is forked in-tree with reqwest optional, so cargo tree finds no ring, rustls or reqwest; HTTP announces use an application-owned bounded transport and https trackers are refused with a visible reason; regular upload slots rank by preference then measured per-peer rate with a 10 s hold, one optimistic slot rotates every 30 s, and the Peers tab shows rates; the scheduler leases several small pieces per peer, taking a 2048-piece 32 MiB localhost scenario from 42.3 s to 5.3 s in release with the payload reservation peak at 1.03 MiB inside the 48 MiB budget. 158 workspace tests and 10 preview tests pass; formatting and Clippy with denied warnings clean; release binary builds. Remaining boundaries: no RTT or reliability scoring, only rates; no resume bitmap, so every launch rechecks from disk; https trackers unsupported until a reviewed pure-Rust TLS stack is chosen; webseed and UPnP unavailable; no headful interaction, clipboard paste, window-close save or Internet swarm test in evidence; download order inside one file follows the seeded tie-break; the rbit fork must be re-applied on any upstream upgrade; Windows optional and untested; perf-results/ is gitignored and local.
